WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.228
Arjun's placement FAQ bot has one question from

2
00:00:03.274 --> 00:00:06.188
a student, and fifty stored answers, each one

3
00:00:06.211 --> 00:00:09.613
a vector. Which answer is closest in meaning?

4
00:00:09.963 --> 00:00:13.713
Picture two vectors as arrows. Cosine similarity measures

5
00:00:13.748 --> 00:00:15.989
the angle between them, and ignores how long

6
00:00:16.035 --> 00:00:17.254
they are.

7
00:00:17.604 --> 00:00:20.599
Same direction scores one. A right angle scores

8
00:00:20.646 --> 00:00:24.442
zero: nothing in common. Opposite directions score minus

9
00:00:24.524 --> 00:00:25.267
one.

10
00:00:25.617 --> 00:00:28.601
Forty-five degrees lands in between, at about zero

11
00:00:28.682 --> 00:00:30.168
point seven one.

12
00:00:30.518 --> 00:00:33.571
The formula is short: the dot product, divided

13
00:00:33.618 --> 00:00:36.369
by the two lengths multiplied together.

14
00:00:36.719 --> 00:00:39.517
Take three, four and six, eight. They point

15
00:00:39.563 --> 00:00:41.874
the same way, so even with different lengths,

16
00:00:41.943 --> 00:00:43.917
the score is exactly one.

17
00:00:44.267 --> 00:00:46.601
Now the trick. Scale every vector to length

18
00:00:46.682 --> 00:00:50.142
one, just once. After that, cosine similarity is

19
00:00:50.176 --> 00:00:51.930
simply the dot product.

20
00:00:52.280 --> 00:00:55.206
So stack all fifty answers into one matrix,

21
00:00:55.566 --> 00:00:57.783
multiply by the question, and you get fifty

22
00:00:57.841 --> 00:00:59.060
scores at once.

23
00:00:59.410 --> 00:01:01.860
Sort them, keep the top three. That is

24
00:01:01.929 --> 00:01:04.565
vector search, in a few lines of NumPy.

25
00:01:04.915 --> 00:01:07.945
The answers are embedded once and saved. Only

26
00:01:07.992 --> 00:01:09.977
the question is new each time.

27
00:01:10.327 --> 00:01:13.299
One warning: a score is for ranking. Zero

28
00:01:13.392 --> 00:01:15.807
point eight two does not mean eighty-two percent

29
00:01:15.865 --> 00:01:16.829
correct.

30
00:01:17.179 --> 00:01:19.431
So the line to remember: vector search is

31
00:01:19.478 --> 00:01:22.125
a dot product and a sort. Everything after

32
00:01:22.171 --> 00:01:24.099
that is about speed.

33
00:01:24.449 --> 00:01:27.050
Next lesson: vector databases.
